  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="payout-title">From order value to bank credit</h2>
      <p>An invented example, to show the shape of a settlement. Your platform's report will have its own lines, and amounts differ.</p>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Sample settlement, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table table-narrow settle">
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Orders shipped, including GST</th><td>₹1,00,000</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Less: returns and refunds</th><td>− ₹8,000</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Less: platform commission and shipping fees</th><td>− ₹15,000</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Less: GST on those fees</th><td>− ₹2,700</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Less: GST TCS and income tax TDS by the platform</th><td>− ₹550</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Less: ads and penalties</th><td>− ₹3,000</td></tr>
            <tr class="settle__total"><th scope="row">Bank credit</th><td>₹70,750</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>What each line means for your books</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Sales</dt><dd>Your return reports what you sold, not what you received.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Commission and fees</dt><dd>These are expenses, and the platform's fee invoices may support input tax credit.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>GST TCS</dt><dd>Section 52 caps this at 1%. A rate of 0.5% is reported following the GST Council's 53rd meeting. It appears in your GST records as a credit once the platform reports it.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>TDS</dt><dd>Income tax deducted by the platform and usable against your tax. Sources point to 0.1% under what was section 194-O. Confirm the current rule.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Returns</dt><dd>Need matching credit notes, and the goods must return to your stock counts.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="month-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="month-title">A monthly reconciliation that works</h2>
    </header>
    <ol class="cycle">
      <li><h3>Download</h3><p>Order, return, settlement and fee invoice reports from each platform.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Tie sales</h3><p>Match orders with your invoices, by state where your GST return requires it.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Tie payouts</h3><p>Match each settlement to the bank credit, and explain each deduction.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Check returns</h3><p>Note those in transit, those received, and those lost or damaged. Claim where you can.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Tie taxes</h3><p>Match TCS with your GST records and TDS with your income tax statement.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Count stock</h3><p>Include what sits in a platform's warehouse, not just your own.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="gst-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="gst-title" class="first-h2">GST questions to ask before you scale</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Do I have to register now?</strong> Selling goods through a platform that collects tax can make registration compulsory regardless of turnover. Some exceptions have been reported, particularly for services and certain small suppliers. Ask before listing.</li>
        <li><strong>What about other states?</strong> Customers elsewhere change how tax is reported. A platform warehouse in another state may require registration there.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I use the Composition Scheme?</strong> Restrictions apply to e-commerce sellers, so check before opting in.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Booking only net payouts,</strong> which hides sales and costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring returns in transit</strong> at month end.</li>
        <li><strong>Not collecting fee invoices,</strong> which leaves credit unclaimed.</li>
        <li><strong>Treating each platform as one customer forever,</strong> without tracking settlements by period.</li>
        <li><strong>Pricing without true per-unit margin,</strong> after fees, returns and ads.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="margin">The number that decides survival</h2>
      <p>Work out your margin per unit after commission, shipping, returns, ads and GST. Many sellers grow sales while losing money. Ask your accountant for a monthly margin by platform, and by your main products if you can.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Which marketplaces have you reconciled, and can you show a redacted sample?</li>
        <li>How do you treat returns, and stock in a platform's warehouse?</li>
        <li>How will you track TCS and TDS credit?</li>
        <li>Do you use a tool for settlements, or do it by hand?</li>
      </ul>

